Cell["\<\
A symmetry that every argument declares is carried over to the result, since \
an entry-by-entry operation treats each index tuple separately. Where the \
arguments disagree nothing is claimed:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ae62acbd-bd61-3043-87a9-c3b333664ca3"],

Cell["\<\
{ArraySymmetry @ ArrayAdd[ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 3}}, \"Symmetric\"],
                           ArrayObject[{{0, 1}, {1, 0}}, \"Symmetric\"]],
  ArraySymmetry @ ArrayAdd[ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 3}}, \"Symmetric\"],
                           ArrayObject[u]]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c6728a1c-5738-7b46-899d-faa9d61d92b9"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"Symmetric\"\>", ",", "\<\"Rigid\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output",Expres\
sionUUID->"e8cb7b7e-786f-1d48-a9b4-c4bb0b0021dc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The arguments are given directly rather than in a list, unlike \
ArrayMultiply, which takes a list of arrays and a specification alongside it. \
A list of arrays is itself an array, so ArrayAdd[{v1, v2}] would be \
ambiguous: it could be the sum of two vectors or the matrix they form. Given \
directly there is nothing to guess."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"4f2b9fe2-e6d1-f34b-82e2-37831dcacbf4"],

Cell["9. Searching for equational identities", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"849ce5ce-0af0-2048-bf60-1bf74eb34e88"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations takes a list of array operations and a list of generic \
arguments, forms every way of nesting the operations over those arguments, \
and returns the equations that hold between them. Operations are ordinary \
functions of arrays, so they are easily built out of ArrayMultiply:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"09200274-8d39-8742-b106-76a0dced10f8"],

Cell["\<\
dot[u_List, w_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w}, \"ij,jk->ik\"];
Fish[u_List, w_List, t_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w, t}, \
\"ijp,qrp,qrk->ijk\"];
BM[u_List, w_List, t_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u, w, t}, \
\"ijp,ipk,pjk->ijk\"];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"6101106f-4439-c342-8fc7-1efd4df60f6d"],

Cell["\<\
Ordinary matrix multiplication on three generic arguments finds \
associativity, and nothing else:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"530b5785-8372-c64f-84f4-db1636775b26"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{dot}, {a1, a2, a3}, \"Order\" -> 2, \"Dimension\" -> 3]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"269f053d-838c-8b4a-92b9-aed900ecc4d7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"3674e541-4a7b-3648-889d-69305dc14c93"],

Cell["\<\
The Fish product on five generic arguments finds a twisted form of ternary \
associativity. One of the two equations below is plain associativity in the \
outer arguments; the other reverses the inner three:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"049b7c31-ee02-a04a-bce9-c2dd9d2de36f"],

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{Fish}, {a1, a2, a3, a4, a5}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"31fdaf25-4b26-2b41-832a-800c2927fb14"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a3", ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a4", ",", "a3", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a3", ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Fish", "[", 
       RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}], ",", "a4", ",", "a5"}], 
     "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"34ec41f1-4773-4842-be3b-\
856ff4360eaf"],

Cell["\<\
The Bhattacharya-Mesner product, on the same five arguments, satisfies \
nothing at all:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f7852dd4-001f-f847-b860-635dc29f08ce"],

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{BM}, {a1, a2, a3, a4, a5}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"1c10a9e1-08a4-ce45-a45f-a9c59ba07dea"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"a9c70580-1c25-7948-940a-df791a8244a4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The search is in two stages, and that is what makes it quick. Every term is \
first evaluated on random integer arrays and only pairs that agree there are \
carried forward. Two terms equal as identities agree on any arrays, so \
nothing correct is ever thrown away; the numerical stage only proposes, and \
the symbolic stage disposes. The surviving candidates are then checked on \
symbolic arrays by expanding the difference of the two sides, which is exact \
for polynomials and far quicker than a general simplification."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"45ad11d2-b002-c84e-8bb7-7933075ef34c"],

Cell["\<\
Whether the arguments may be permuted is an option. With a fixed order only \
the nesting varies, which for matrix multiplication makes no difference, \
since associativity does not move its arguments:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"247515e1-dbdf-4647-9022-958685090e46"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{dot}, {a1, a2, a3}, \"Order\" -> 2, \"Dimension\" -> 3,
                    \"Permute\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"d8871dfc-b1fd-cc46-bdd5-f1f971716bd9"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], ",", "a3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"47303d06-083e-994c-96ba-161a38bc885a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "An identity verified on symbolic arrays of a given dimension is proved at \
that dimension, not at every dimension. The default is 2, which is small and \
quick; raising \"Dimension\" checks more widely. The Fish equations above \
come out the same at dimension 3, which is reassuring without being a proof \
in general."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"f2448d7d-8100-ca45-98e4-6a15138ec4df"],

Cell["More than one operation at a time", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"6ca99f33-e571-2842-895c-7a308bcdf857"],

Cell["\<\
Operations of different arities may be searched together. A transpose is \
unary and a matrix product binary; on two generic arguments they give the law \
that transposing a product reverses it, and the law that transposing twice \
does nothing:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"85dac7fe-e0b2-3845-af35-9615976f7b8a"],

Cell["\<\
perp[u_List] := Normal @ ArrayMultiply[{u}, \"ij->ji\"];
FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e5c1f2a9-6377-f34c-9b29-37a51aa87e06"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"a1", "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expression\
UUID->"2ebcc6b0-c7ea-ac41-bde9-e590c955c5af"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "An operation of arity one is the exception to the rule that a term uses \
exactly the given arguments. It takes a term to a term of the same shape, so \
it may be applied over and over at any point without consuming an argument, \
and every term is offered wrapped in up to \"UnaryDepth\" nested unary \
operations, two by default. That is what brings the involutivity within \
reach: it needs the transpose twice over on one argument, where the \
anti-homomorphism law uses it once on each side."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"7c0095b3-f490-e14a-b0c5-a7025ccac547"],

Cell["\<\
Setting the depth to one puts the involutivity back out of reach \[LongDash] \
and with it gone, the remaining law is reported in each of its forms instead \
of once, since without perp[perp[a]] == a there is nothing to rewrite one \
form into another:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7f7897ca-e54e-3f4a-82e4-604f3d464bf0"],

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"UnaryDepth\" -> 1]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e0996db5-01e4-d340-9141-91819146969c"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a2", ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a1", ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
     RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a2", "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",\
ExpressionUUID->"3da05cf6-9931-5548-830f-bf9733c94c14"],

Cell["\<\
The anti-homomorphism law moves its arguments, so holding them in the order \
given leaves only the involutivity, which does not:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8a51fc1a-5d86-024f-9aa1-dafaad13a2b1"],

Cell["\<\
FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Permute\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"bc189c52-b0b3-de43-91b9-b0591b83adc9"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"a1", "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"perp", "[", 
    RowBox[{"perp", "[", "a1", "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUID\
->"67977940-c7e9-df48-845a-af19bf804c49"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "Freely nested unary operations bring a flood with them. Every consequence \
of those two laws is an equation between terms of the search, and each would \
be reported in its own right: a hundred and more of them here, all saying the \
same two things over again. So an equation that follows from the others is \
left out. Each equation is read as a schema, its arguments standing for \
arbitrary terms, and turned into a rewrite rule that fires only when it makes \
a term strictly smaller; any number of such rules is then a terminating \
rewriting system, and an equation whose two sides rewrite to a common form is \
a consequence of the ones already kept. \"Reduce\" -> False reports \
everything found."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"8dd08b98-4176-dd41-9f60-40bf028dbf54"],

Cell["\<\
Length @ FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Reduce\" -> False]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"19c797c9-9ca5-e848-b28a-c32bfdef9259"],

Cell[BoxData["115"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"cb7b7650-4a65-f94c-9fc3-60a64fa82cd4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "What survives is a basis, not a canonical set. Which of several equivalent \
forms of a law is kept depends on the order the equations are considered in \
\[LongDash] the anti-homomorphism law above is reported with the transposes \
gathered on whichever side the ordering favours \[LongDash] and the reduction \
is by rewriting alone, so a consequence that rewriting cannot reach is still \
reported. A genuinely minimal set of axioms is the business of Knuth-Bendix \
completion, which this is not."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"2b80b61f-4db3-174d-a11f-8bb9ac709a99"],

Cell["\<\
A trace and a matrix product give the cyclic property of the trace:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d561442c-e50c-1347-af28-55d9a40eff44"],

Cell["FindArrayEquations[{trace, dot}, {a1, a2}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e5d8b6c2-a50e-be4a-ac00-027ca9ad4819"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"trace", "[", 
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a1", ",", "a2"}], "]"}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
   RowBox[{"trace", "[", 
    RowBox[{"dot", "[", 
     RowBox[{"a2", ",", "a1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",ExpressionUUI\
D->"d04061eb-23ff-8547-af97-2d102c204a79"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The trace returns a scalar, so a nesting that feeds it back into a product \
cannot evaluate. Such a term is dropped rather than compared, which is what \
lets an operation of that shape sit at the top of a term without having to be \
excluded by hand."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"fbb4fef4-2df1-2e4d-8493-0ef6071dfda7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "Neither of those searches was told the shape of its test arrays. With \
\"Order\" -> Automatic, the default, the smallest order on which every \
operation actually evaluates is used, which is 2 for matrix operations and 3 \
for the ternary array products. Operations are shape-specific enough that \
this is a search for the shape they are defined on rather than a guess."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"c25bee08-2daf-6340-b817-d7bde93b2223"],

Cell["\<\
Forcing a shape the operations cannot take says so, rather than coming back \
empty:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4ad6391f-daba-d44e-8ea1-3441ccb7a3e0"],

Cell["\<\
Quiet @ FindArrayEquations[{perp, dot}, {a1, a2}, \"Order\" -> 4]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"917f64e3-2304-be4c-b0d3-473cce47976c"],

Cell[BoxData["$Failed"], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"2c74200e-2751-e248-91a2-a80af5241927"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Design note.  ",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "An empty result and a result that could not be computed are different \
answers, and conflating them would be the worst kind of quiet failure: it \
would read as a proof that no identity exists. Whenever no nesting evaluates, \
FindArrayEquations::eval is issued and $Failed returned."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{2, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameMargins->{{10, 0}, {6, 6}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.7],
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.25],ExpressionUUID->"f99a7ea6-6f71-5544-b003-5d5ca556ebbb"],

Cell["10. The adjacency hypermatrix", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"41cd9316-73f1-e84a-941c-a3ec0e021c12"],

Cell["\<\
The two halves of the package meet here. A hypergraph is a multiset of edges, \
each with an arity and a symmetry type; an array has an order and a symmetry. \
The two vocabularies line up one for one, and the passage from a hypergraph \
to a hypermatrix is what that correspondence looks like when it is written \
down.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7a3f5ca4-85c9-0c40-9c31-7ec2caf290c6"],

Cell["\<\
hAdj = Hypergraph[{Edge[{1, 2, 3}, \"Unordered\"], Edge[{3, 4}, \
\"Directed\"],
                   Edge[{1, 2}, \"Directed\"]}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"212fe8a1-bf3f-c340-9a90-70f565af98c1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 GraphicsBox[
  NamespaceBox["Hypergraphs",
   DynamicModuleBox[{Typeset`hg = HoldComplete[
     WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Hypergraph[{
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[1], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[2], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[3], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[4]}, {
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Edge[{
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[1], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[2]}, "Directed"], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Edge[{
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[3], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[4]}, "Directed"], 
       WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Edge[{
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[1], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[2], 
         WolframInstitute`Hypergraphs`Vertex[3]}, "Unordered"]}]]}, 
    {AbsoluteThickness[Medium], Opacity[0.5], Arrowheads[{{Medium, 0.5}}], {
      {Opacity[1], Arrowheads[{{Small, 0.5}}], 
